  While I'm in no position to impugn those who have ascended with
"accidental" conduct, never having ascended myself, I believe I can
shed some light on the question. Most people seem to respond to it
with "Well, if you choose it from the start, it's just more
satisfying." While this may be true, perhaps a stronger argument can
be found in the game's own wording. When you finish, The conducts are
listed this way:

Voluntary challenges:

You went without food.
...

  The use of the word "voluntary" would seem to imply that the DevTeam
itself does not consider an accidental conduct legitimate (or should
consider using a different word). Therefore, the conduct preselection
patch sounds like quite a good idea to me. Maybe in the future, the
DevTeam should make the game distinguish between "Voluntary
challenges" and "Streaks of misfortune," or something. The
impossibility of implementing this flawlessly this has already been
discussed, I see, since how can the game tell what an "opportunity"
for genocide is? You may have had a magic marker with enough charges
to write geno and decided not to, but how can the game tell whether it
would've done you any good anyway?

  I think the answer to your question is that one, three, and possibly
four (since the player did still make a decision to go genoless in
spite of having geno scrolls) all count.

  BTW, "Streaks of misfortune" could be an interesting category in its
own right. For instance, "rubbed X magic lamps, but never got a wish,"
"sat on X thrones without receiving any  benefits," "Fort Ludios was
never generated," "never found a tinning kit," "No coaligned priest
was never generated," etc.

